\section{Related Work}


In this section, we review prior work on conversational agents (CAs) that support behavior change, with a focus on how these systems shape users' planning, reflection, and everyday work practices.
We organize our review around a conceptual framework that guides our study, which consists of four dimensions: (1) planning and reflection, which describe how conversations help users articulate and evaluate their daily activities; (2) social interpretation of the agent, which influences how users perceive and respond to CA behaviors; (3) cooperation, which captures how users and agents work to maintain shared understanding during interaction; and (4) proactivity, which concerns how agents initiate and guide conversations rather than waiting for user input.
