\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Recent years have seen remarkable progress in the code generation capabilities of language models (LMs), driven by an increase in model size, training data, and improvements in overall training methodologies~\citep{grattafiori2024llama, achiam2023gpt, nijkamp2023codegen2, li2023starcoder, fried2022incoder, roziere2023code, achiam2023gpt, touvron2023llama}. Yet, despite these advances, LMs continue to struggle with basic syntactic tasks such as generating balanced parentheses and correct indentation~\citep{dou2024s, wang2024large}. The failure of LMs to perform these seemingly simple tasks stands in stark contrast to their improved performance on more complex coding benchmarks~\citep{jain2024livecodebench, chen2021codex, austin2021program}, raising an important interpretability question: \emph{How do LMs internally compute predictions for syntactic tasks and why do these computations sometimes fail?} 

% LMs' susceptibility to failure on basic syntactic tasks poses both a practical challenge for reliable code generation and an interpretability gap in understanding how these models internally represent and execute syntactic completion tasks. 
